WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.920 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.350 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:19.130 align:middle line:90%


00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:30.270 align:middle line:84%
CASEY RODRIGUEZ: OK, so
the main character today,

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:36.580 align:middle line:84%
in this lecture and the
next, are compact operators,

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:40.800 align:middle line:84%
which I introduced at
the end of last lecture.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:57.230 align:middle line:84%
And I'll recall throughout,
H is a Hilbert space.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:01.740 align:middle line:90%


00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:11.130 align:middle line:84%
So we say an operator,
let's call it K,

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:20.080 align:middle line:84%
is a bounded linear operator,
is a compact operator,

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:31.590 align:middle line:84%
if the image of the unit ball,
taking the closure of that--

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:38.060 align:middle line:84%
so this is just a set of
all elements K u with u

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:53.140 align:middle line:84%
less than or equal to 1
closure is compact in H.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:58.750 align:middle line:84%
And how did this set
of operators come up?

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.880 align:middle line:84%
At the end of last
time, we dealt

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.430 align:middle line:84%
with finite rank
operators, which

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:04.930 align:middle line:90%
were just basically matrices.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:08.740 align:middle line:84%
You can actually write them
so that they're completely

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:13.360 align:middle line:84%
described with respect to a
certain basis as a matrix.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.880 align:middle line:84%
And the question we posed at
the end of the last lecture

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.930 align:middle line:84%
was, these finite
rank operators,

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.610 align:middle line:84%
they form a subspace
of the space

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.080 align:middle line:90%
of all bounded linear operators.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.310 align:middle line:90%
Is that subspace closed?

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.600 align:middle line:90%
And the answer is no.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.370 align:middle line:84%
What is the closure
of the finite rank

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:31.810 align:middle line:84%
operators, which
we know and love

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.610 align:middle line:90%
because they're just matrices?

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.950 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll prove
today is that it's exactly

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.440 align:middle line:90%
the space of compact operators.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.930 align:middle line:90%
But let me just--

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:46.510 align:middle line:84%
last time, I gave
you an example of one

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:53.330 align:middle line:84%
that is a compact
operator K a equal to,

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:58.640 align:middle line:84%
let's say, a1 divided by
a1 one, a2 divided by 2,

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:06.110 align:middle line:84%
a3 divided by 3, and so on,
defined for a in l2, little l2.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:13.440 align:middle line:84%
Then my claim is that K
is a compact operator.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.440 align:middle line:84%
And in the assignment, you
show basically a variety

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.050 align:middle line:84%
of other operators that
come up quite often

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.310 align:middle line:90%
are compact operators.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:26.340 align:middle line:84%
For example, you also
show, essentially,

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:39.270 align:middle line:84%
that if K is a continuous
function on 0, 1, cross 0, 1,

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:43.350 align:middle line:84%
and I define a
linear operator T f

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:48.750 align:middle line:84%
of x to be the integral
from 0 to 1 of K x, y,

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:56.730 align:middle line:84%
f of y dy, now f is
in big L2 of 0, 1.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:01.680 align:middle line:84%
Then part of the assignment
is to use and remind yourself

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.110 align:middle line:90%
of one of the only--

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.490 align:middle line:90%
one of the very few--

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.160 align:middle line:84%
compactness results
one has, is to use

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.750 align:middle line:84%
the Arzela-Ascoli theorem
to show that this is,

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.790 align:middle line:90%
in fact, a compact operator.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:15.360 align:middle line:84%
So from the assignment,
you'll prove

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:24.790 align:middle line:90%
T is compact operator on L2.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:27.800 align:middle line:90%


00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.640 align:middle line:84%
Now, maybe you think
I'm just coming up

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.140 align:middle line:84%
with something that
looks like this

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:35.930 align:middle line:84%
to come up with examples
of a compact operator,

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:42.590 align:middle line:84%
but the solution operator
for differential equations

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.630 align:middle line:90%
take this form.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:47.420 align:middle line:90%
So for example--

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.170 align:middle line:84%
I guess this would
be an example,

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:50.940 align:middle line:90%
as part of this example--

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.340 align:middle line:90%
if I take K x, y to be--

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.920 align:middle line:84%
let's see if I
can get it right--

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:06.700 align:middle line:84%
x minus 1 times y,
x times y minus 1,

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:11.140 align:middle line:84%
for 0 less than or
equal to y less than

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.030 align:middle line:84%
or equal to x less
than or equal to 1.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:17.195 align:middle line:90%
And then now reverse.

00:05:17.195 --> 00:05:22.190 align:middle line:90%


00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:22.750 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.270 align:middle line:90%


00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:27.060 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I think that's it.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.720 align:middle line:90%


00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:35.120 align:middle line:90%
Then u equals 0, 1 K of x, y.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:36.290 align:middle line:90%
You can actually compute.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:41.460 align:middle line:90%


00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:44.490 align:middle line:84%
Solves the
differentiable equation

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:56.250 align:middle line:84%
u double prime equals f, u of 0
equals u of 1 equals 0 on 0, 1.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:01.650 align:middle line:84%
So maybe this has
a minus in front.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:03.030 align:middle line:90%
I can't exactly remember--

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:07.170 align:middle line:90%


00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.930 align:middle line:90%
u of x.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:16.320 align:middle line:84%
So the solution operator,
meaning if my input is f,

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.900 align:middle line:84%
then the solution to this
differential equation

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:19.740 align:middle line:90%
is actually--

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.570 align:middle line:84%
I can write it as
a compact operator

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:23.670 align:middle line:90%
acting on the data, which is f.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:28.760 align:middle line:90%


00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.310 align:middle line:84%
So these don't just
pop up out of nowhere.

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:35.240 align:middle line:90%
They pop up quite naturally.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:41.240 align:middle line:84%
Now, these are a bunch of
examples on compact operators.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:45.280 align:middle line:84%
One non-example-- it's
easy to actually come up

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.740 align:middle line:84%
with non-examples of
compact operators--

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:54.430 align:middle line:84%
but let's say the
identity on little l2.

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:56.200 align:middle line:90%
This is not a compact operator.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:03.040 align:middle line:90%


00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:03.790 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:08.860 align:middle line:84%
Because the condition of
being a compact operator

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:12.670 align:middle line:84%
is that K of the
unit ball should be

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:16.570 align:middle line:90%
a compact subset of little l2.

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:24.180 align:middle line:84%
But let's let e sub
n be the sequence--

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.400 align:middle line:84%
our favorite
sequence-- which is 0,

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.440 align:middle line:84%
except in the n-th
spot, where it's 1.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:34.280 align:middle line:90%


00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:39.220 align:middle line:90%
Then each of these has norm 1.

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:42.310 align:middle line:90%


00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:51.450 align:middle line:84%
And so if I look at the
image, e n, that's I e m,

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:53.340 align:middle line:84%
and I compute the
square of that,

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:56.970 align:middle line:84%
you can see quite easily
this is equal to 2

00:07:56.970 --> 00:08:02.940 align:middle line:84%
since these are orthonormal
for all n not equal to m.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:19.530 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, I e n does not
have a convergent subsequence.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:22.530 align:middle line:84%
If I, the identity, was
a compact operator, then

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:26.610 align:middle line:84%
the image of the closed
unit ball in little l2

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:29.130 align:middle line:84%
should be a compact
set, meaning what?

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:34.710 align:middle line:84%
Meaning elements of
this form, I evaluated

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:37.409 align:middle line:84%
on something that has norm
less than or equal to 1,

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:41.650 align:middle line:90%
should form a compact set.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:44.440 align:middle line:84%
So if I have a sequence
of guys in the unit ball,

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:46.570 align:middle line:84%
closed unit ball,
then when I hits it,

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:51.590 align:middle line:84%
I should be able to find
a convergent subsequence.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.410 align:middle line:84%
But here, if you
just compute, then we

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:59.800 align:middle line:84%
have the distance between
any two of these guys for n

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:00.880 align:middle line:90%
not equal to m is 2.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:02.950 align:middle line:84%
So this sequence
can't ever be Cauchy,

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.440 align:middle line:84%
nor can any subsequence
ever be Cauchy.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.590 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, this has
no convergent subsequence.

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:12.640 align:middle line:84%
So the identity on little l2
is not a compact operator.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:23.150 align:middle line:84%
And by this argument,
you can, in fact,

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:25.730 align:middle line:84%
prove that the identity
is not a compact operator

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:28.055 align:middle line:84%
on any infinite-dimensional
Hilbert space.

00:09:28.055 --> 00:09:35.450 align:middle line:90%


00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:41.610 align:middle line:84%
So we have one way of
showing that an operator is

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:45.510 align:middle line:84%
a compact operator, which is
by verifying the definition.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.380 align:middle line:84%
And then we now
have this theorem,

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.740 align:middle line:84%
which I alluded to
at the start, showing

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:55.140 align:middle line:84%
that the set of
compact operators

00:09:55.140 --> 00:10:00.470 align:middle line:84%
is the closure of the
finite rank operators.

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:14.130 align:middle line:84%
So let H be a separable
Hilbert space.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:18.850 align:middle line:90%


00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:27.850 align:middle line:84%
Then, an operator T, bounded
linear operator T on H,

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:36.820 align:middle line:84%
is compact operator if and only
if there exists a sequence T

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:49.210 align:middle line:84%
n of finite rank ops,
which converge to T

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:50.830 align:middle line:90%
n the operator norm.

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:57.390 align:middle line:90%


00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:00.620 align:middle line:84%
So this proves that the
set of compact operators

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:06.320 align:middle line:84%
is the closure of the set of
finite rank operators, which

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:07.295 align:middle line:90%
we denoted by--

00:11:07.295 --> 00:11:09.940 align:middle line:90%


00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:14.030 align:middle line:84%
so this shows
compact operators is

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:16.310 align:middle line:84%
the closure of the finite
rank operators, which

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:30.270 align:middle line:90%
we denoted by R. That's R of H.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:42.020 align:middle line:84%
So first direction
is to show that if I

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:43.580 align:middle line:84%
have a compact
operator, then it's

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:49.480 align:middle line:84%
equal to a norm limit of
finite rank operators.

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:54.055 align:middle line:84%
So since H is separable, it
has an orthonormal basis.

00:11:54.055 --> 00:12:05.110 align:middle line:90%


00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:22.390 align:middle line:84%
Now, since T is a
compact operator,

00:12:22.390 --> 00:12:25.840 align:middle line:84%
remember this means
that the image

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:32.590 align:middle line:84%
T u with of all those elements
of norm less than or equal to 1

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:48.785 align:middle line:84%
is contained in a compact set,
namely the closure of this set.

00:12:48.785 --> 00:12:51.380 align:middle line:90%


00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:57.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, since this set is contained
in a compact set, I can use--

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:12.280 align:middle line:84%
in fact, let me just
put it's a compact set.

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:13.780 align:middle line:84%
And so we have this
characterization

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:18.070 align:middle line:84%
of compact subsets of a
separable Hilbert space

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:23.170 align:middle line:84%
by being closed, bounded,
and having equismall tails.

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:29.660 align:middle line:84%
So then, for every
epsilon positive,

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:33.550 align:middle line:84%
there exists a
natural number N such

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:51.880 align:middle line:84%
that if I look at sum over K
bigger than N, K u e K squared,

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:58.000 align:middle line:84%
this is less than epsilon
squared for all elements

00:13:58.000 --> 00:13:59.680 align:middle line:84%
with norm less
than or equal to 1.

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:02.740 align:middle line:84%
Because the K u's-- these
are in this compact--

00:14:02.740 --> 00:14:03.990 align:middle line:90%
why am I using K?

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:06.340 align:middle line:90%
Should use T, sorry.

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:08.620 align:middle line:84%
The T u's are all
in this compact set.

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:10.840 align:middle line:84%
So we have this
equismall tail condition

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:13.210 align:middle line:84%
that for every
epsilon, we can find

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:17.170 align:middle line:84%
a N which does not depend on
the elements of the compact set,

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:21.175 align:middle line:84%
so that the tails of the
Fourier coefficients are small.

00:14:21.175 --> 00:14:26.450 align:middle line:90%


00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:28.820 align:middle line:84%
So what this says is
that we can basically,

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:32.030 align:middle line:84%
if we're taking any u with
norm less than or equal to 1

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:40.190 align:middle line:84%
and hitting it with T, then the
end of the Fourier coefficients

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:41.610 align:middle line:90%
don't really matter.

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.580 align:middle line:84%
And so we should think
we can approximate T

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:47.630 align:middle line:84%
by just a finite rank operator
that consists of coefficients

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:50.750 align:middle line:84%
like this times e k
for k between 1 to N.

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:53.790 align:middle line:84%
And that's basically
what we're going to do.

00:14:53.790 --> 00:15:07.630 align:middle line:84%
So for n, define T n of u to
be sum from k equals 1 to n

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:12.430 align:middle line:90%
of T u e k e k.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:19.410 align:middle line:90%


00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:24.660 align:middle line:84%
And this is for u
in H. So one can

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:30.030 align:middle line:84%
check that this is a
bounded linear operator

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.320 align:middle line:90%
and it's also a finite rank.

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.015 align:middle line:90%
Because the range is--

00:15:33.015 --> 00:15:37.630 align:middle line:90%


00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:40.210 align:middle line:84%
so it's clearly a
bounded linear operator.

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:44.065 align:middle line:84%
Just take the norm
squared of this.

00:15:44.065 --> 00:15:46.312 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to the sum
of squares of the Fourier

00:15:46.312 --> 00:15:48.520 align:middle line:84%
coefficients, which is less
than or equal to the norm

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:51.370 align:middle line:84%
of 2 squared, which is less
than or equal to the norm

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:52.370 align:middle line:90%
of T squared.

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:56.270 align:middle line:84%
So it's clearly a
bounded linear operator.

00:15:56.270 --> 00:16:00.670 align:middle line:84%
And if I look at
the range of T n,

00:16:00.670 --> 00:16:06.190 align:middle line:84%
this is a subset of the
span of e1 up to e n.

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:13.490 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, T n is a finite
rank operator for all n.

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:16.750 align:middle line:90%


00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:21.970 align:middle line:84%
So I claim this is our set
of finite rank operators

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:30.310 align:middle line:84%
that converges to T
as n goes to infinity.

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:35.940 align:middle line:84%
So to prove this, we'll just
do standard epsilon N argument.

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:38.790 align:middle line:90%
Let epsilon be positive.

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:50.200 align:middle line:84%
Then let N be the N that appears
in this condition star up here,

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:56.980 align:middle line:84%
meaning capital N is the natural
numbers, guaranteeing this.

00:16:56.980 --> 00:17:01.470 align:middle line:90%


00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:04.040 align:middle line:84%
So now, I claim that for
all little n bigger than

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:08.060 align:middle line:84%
or equal to this capital N, the
operator norm of T n minus T

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:10.610 align:middle line:84%
is less than or
equal to epsilon,

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:11.735 align:middle line:90%
which is still good enough.

00:17:11.735 --> 00:17:20.660 align:middle line:90%


00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:25.790 align:middle line:84%
Then for all-- then
if norm of u equals 1,

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:31.482 align:middle line:84%
we compute that T
n of u minus T u.

00:17:31.482 --> 00:17:35.690 align:middle line:90%


00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:39.290 align:middle line:84%
So we haven't used anything
about this e k yet,

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:42.380 align:middle line:84%
but this is where we use that
they're in orthonormal basis,

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:44.960 align:middle line:90%
is that I can write T n of u.

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:47.360 align:middle line:84%
Well, first off, this just
is from the definition

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:49.430 align:middle line:90%
that this is K equals 1 to n--

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:53.120 align:middle line:90%
and let me put a square here--

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:59.130 align:middle line:90%
of T u E k E k minus--

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:06.590 align:middle line:84%
and now I expand this
element as in this basis T u

00:18:06.590 --> 00:18:10.685 align:middle line:90%
e k e k squared.

00:18:10.685 --> 00:18:13.760 align:middle line:90%


00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:17.190 align:middle line:84%
You see what cancels out
are the first n of these.

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:20.430 align:middle line:90%


00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:30.036 align:middle line:84%
And this equals the norm squared
of k bigger than n of T u e k

00:18:30.036 --> 00:18:33.070 align:middle line:90%
e k norm squared.

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:38.340 align:middle line:84%
And this is equal to,
since these are orthogonal,

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:39.840 align:middle line:90%
k bigger than e--

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:48.430 align:middle line:84%
orthonormal, i mean-- k
bigger than n T u j k squared.

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:52.410 align:middle line:84%
And now, n is bigger than
or equal to capital N.

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:56.070 align:middle line:84%
So this is less
than or equal to k

00:18:56.070 --> 00:19:02.820 align:middle line:84%
bigger than N T u e
k squared, and that's

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:04.000 align:middle line:90%
less than epsilon squared.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:09.870 align:middle line:84%
So what we've shown is
that for all u of norm 1,

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:14.430 align:middle line:84%
T N u minus T u squared is
less than epsilon squared.

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:19.800 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, if I take the
sup over all u that have norm 1,

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:23.580 align:middle line:84%
I get that the norm of T n
minus T, which is that sup,

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:27.280 align:middle line:84%
is less than or
equal to epsilon.

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:37.300 align:middle line:84%
So thus, the T n's converge
to T in operator norm.

00:19:37.300 --> 00:19:47.170 align:middle line:90%


00:19:47.170 --> 00:19:49.630 align:middle line:84%
So now, going in the
opposite direction,

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:55.590 align:middle line:84%
we will use this
second characterization

00:19:55.590 --> 00:20:00.120 align:middle line:84%
that we have of compact
sets that we didn't prove,

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:02.460 align:middle line:84%
but I said you should
look up if you want to.

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:04.360 align:middle line:84%
It's, again, a
diagonalization argument,

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:07.710 align:middle line:84%
but that says that compact
subsets can be approximated

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:10.200 align:middle line:90%
by finite dimensional--

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:14.480 align:middle line:90%
or are sets that are compact--

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:16.460 align:middle line:84%
or a set is compact
if and only if it

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:22.070 align:middle line:84%
can be approximated by
finite-dimensional subspaces.

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:27.590 align:middle line:84%
So now, we're proving
the opposite direction.

00:20:27.590 --> 00:20:37.780 align:middle line:84%
And suppose that T can be
approximated in operator norm

00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:40.270 align:middle line:84%
by a sequence of
finite rank operators.

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:47.650 align:middle line:90%


00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:50.020 align:middle line:84%
And we want to show that
T is a compact operator.

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:58.390 align:middle line:90%


00:20:58.390 --> 00:21:07.800 align:middle line:84%
So first, note that if I
look at T u norm less than

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:09.880 align:middle line:90%
or equal to 1 closure--

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:12.450 align:middle line:84%
so this is the set
that I'm trying

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:16.380 align:middle line:84%
to show is a compact set-- it's
obviously closed because it's

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:18.090 align:middle line:90%
the closure of a set.

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:21.240 align:middle line:84%
The closure of a set is
always the smallest closed set

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:23.210 align:middle line:90%
containing that set.

00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:30.320 align:middle line:84%
And it's contained because
T is a bounded operator.

00:21:30.320 --> 00:21:33.860 align:middle line:84%
It's always contained
in the set of,

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:38.090 align:middle line:84%
let's call it v in H,
where the norm of v

00:21:38.090 --> 00:21:43.460 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to the norm of T.

00:21:43.460 --> 00:22:01.690 align:middle line:84%
So this shows that the set
here is closed and bounded.

00:22:01.690 --> 00:22:09.290 align:middle line:90%


00:22:09.290 --> 00:22:11.870 align:middle line:84%
So to conclude that this
set is compact, we just

00:22:11.870 --> 00:22:13.820 align:middle line:84%
need to show that it
can be approximated

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:17.640 align:middle line:84%
by finite-dimensional subspaces,
meaning for every epsilon,

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:19.680 align:middle line:84%
there exists a
finite-dimensional subspace

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:23.060 align:middle line:84%
so that the distance
from every element

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:28.655 align:middle line:84%
in this set to the subspace
W is less than epsilon.

00:22:28.655 --> 00:22:33.040 align:middle line:90%


00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:43.090 align:middle line:84%
So we want to show now,
or we make this a claim,

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:48.130 align:middle line:84%
for all epsilon
positive, there exists

00:22:48.130 --> 00:23:02.820 align:middle line:84%
a finite-dimensional subspace
W such that for all u less than

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:11.480 align:middle line:84%
or equal to 1, we have that the
distance from this element T

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:19.100 align:middle line:90%
u to w is less than epsilon.

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:23.330 align:middle line:84%
That's what I mean by we
can approximate this set

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:27.200 align:middle line:90%
by finite-dimensional subspaces.

00:23:27.200 --> 00:23:31.370 align:middle line:84%
Once we can do that, then
we've proven the claim,

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:33.890 align:middle line:84%
or we can use that
characterization

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:37.850 align:middle line:84%
of compact sets to conclude
that this set is compact.

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:39.860 align:middle line:90%
Now, what's the idea?

00:23:39.860 --> 00:23:43.850 align:middle line:84%
We have that T, which
again, this set here is just

00:23:43.850 --> 00:23:48.780 align:middle line:84%
the image of the closed unit
ball by T taking the closure.

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:52.280 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, T can be
approximated by a finite rank

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:53.000 align:middle line:90%
operator.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:54.990 align:middle line:90%
Its range is finite dimensional.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:58.880 align:middle line:84%
So this set should
be approximated

00:23:58.880 --> 00:24:03.840 align:middle line:84%
by the range of T n, which
is finite dimensional,

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:06.390 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, should
give us our claim.

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:08.290 align:middle line:90%
And that's exactly what we do.

00:24:08.290 --> 00:24:16.390 align:middle line:84%
So since the norm of
T n minus T goes to 0,

00:24:16.390 --> 00:24:20.110 align:middle line:84%
there exists a
natural number N such

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:25.625 align:middle line:84%
that I have that for
all little n bigger

00:24:25.625 --> 00:24:27.500 align:middle line:84%
than or equal to capital
N, blah, blah, blah.

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:29.260 align:middle line:90%
But I don't need all little n.

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:32.485 align:middle line:84%
I just have this
less than epsilon.

00:24:32.485 --> 00:24:40.007 align:middle line:90%


00:24:40.007 --> 00:24:47.150 align:middle line:84%
Let W be the range
of T sub n, which is

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:48.455 align:middle line:90%
a finite-dimensional subspace.

00:24:48.455 --> 00:24:57.400 align:middle line:90%


00:24:57.400 --> 00:25:00.720 align:middle line:84%
And now, I want to show that
I have this condition here--

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:03.210 align:middle line:84%
that for all u with norm
less than or equal to 1,

00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:08.152 align:middle line:84%
this distance from T u to
capital W is less than--

00:25:08.152 --> 00:25:10.110 align:middle line:84%
we can make that less
than or equal to epsilon.

00:25:10.110 --> 00:25:13.170 align:middle line:84%
That still proves
what we want, just

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:15.450 align:middle line:84%
go through and change
epsilon to epsilon over 2.

00:25:15.450 --> 00:25:19.010 align:middle line:90%


00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:26.240 align:middle line:84%
Then, for all elements in H with
norm less than or equal to 1,

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:32.690 align:middle line:84%
if I take T u and
subtract off T n u, which

00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:39.820 align:middle line:84%
is an element in
capital W, this is

00:25:39.820 --> 00:25:43.630 align:middle line:90%
less than or equal to T minus--

00:25:43.630 --> 00:25:46.600 align:middle line:84%
so the operator
norm of T minus T

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:53.590 align:middle line:84%
n times norm of u, which is
less than or equal to T minus T

00:25:53.590 --> 00:25:57.180 align:middle line:90%
n, which is less than epsilon.

00:25:57.180 --> 00:26:03.010 align:middle line:90%


00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:04.760 align:middle line:84%
Put a less than there--
it doesn't matter.

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:07.470 align:middle line:90%


00:26:07.470 --> 00:26:11.030 align:middle line:84%
So this holds for one
particular element of W,

00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:17.210 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, the n-th,
which is the distance from T u

00:26:17.210 --> 00:26:20.642 align:middle line:84%
to capital W, which is
less than or equal to this,

00:26:20.642 --> 00:26:21.725 align:middle line:90%
must be less than epsilon.

00:26:21.725 --> 00:26:25.010 align:middle line:90%


00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:30.600 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, this
proves the claim.

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:34.973 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that
this set is compact

00:26:34.973 --> 00:26:36.390 align:middle line:84%
follows from that
characterization

00:26:36.390 --> 00:26:39.250 align:middle line:84%
of compact sets,
which we proved--

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:42.520 align:middle line:84%
or didn't prove, but at least
stated a few lectures ago.

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:46.810 align:middle line:90%


00:26:46.810 --> 00:26:49.680 align:middle line:84%
So I mean, you can use the
definition to verify something

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:51.390 align:middle line:90%
as a compact operator.

00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:54.840 align:middle line:84%
You could use this
alternative characterization

00:26:54.840 --> 00:27:00.210 align:middle line:84%
as a limit in operator norm
of finite rank operators

00:27:00.210 --> 00:27:03.960 align:middle line:84%
to verify something,
say compact operator.

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:06.540 align:middle line:90%


00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:09.630 align:middle line:84%
It's just whatever's most
convenient at the time.

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:12.420 align:middle line:90%
So how about the end of--

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:14.340 align:middle line:84%
when we talked about
finite rank operators,

00:27:14.340 --> 00:27:19.200 align:middle line:84%
we also went a little bit into
their algebraic structure.

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:21.030 align:middle line:84%
We can do the same
for compact operators.

00:27:21.030 --> 00:27:31.790 align:middle line:84%
So again, let H be a
separable Hilbert space.

00:27:31.790 --> 00:27:37.520 align:middle line:84%
Then the first property of
compact operators is that--

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:50.150 align:middle line:84%
and let's call it a name, K of H
be the set of compact operators

00:27:50.150 --> 00:27:53.030 align:middle line:90%
on H.

00:27:53.030 --> 00:28:02.980 align:middle line:84%
And the first is
K of H is a closed

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:10.990 align:middle line:84%
subspace of the space of bounded
linear operators on the Hilbert

00:28:10.990 --> 00:28:12.380 align:middle line:90%
space.

00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:14.680 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that it's
a linear subspace

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:17.215 align:middle line:90%
is not too difficult to prove.

00:28:17.215 --> 00:28:19.090 align:middle line:84%
The fact that it's closed
follows immediately

00:28:19.090 --> 00:28:20.840 align:middle line:84%
from what we've done
so far when we showed

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:23.980 align:middle line:84%
that the compact operators
are the closure of the set

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:26.943 align:middle line:90%
of finite rank operators.

00:28:26.943 --> 00:28:28.360 align:middle line:84%
Really, I mean the
fact that we've

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:30.310 align:middle line:84%
shown that proves both of
these, because the closure

00:28:30.310 --> 00:28:31.925 align:middle line:84%
of a subspace is,
again, a subspace.

00:28:31.925 --> 00:28:39.120 align:middle line:90%


00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:44.430 align:middle line:84%
If T is a compact
operator, then its adjoint

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:46.230 align:middle line:90%
is also a compact operator.

00:28:46.230 --> 00:28:49.380 align:middle line:90%


00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:58.220 align:middle line:84%
And for every pair of
bounded linear operators

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:03.500 align:middle line:84%
and compact operator, if
I multiply on the left

00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:09.050 align:middle line:84%
and on the right by these
bounded linear operators,

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:14.160 align:middle line:90%
that remains a compact operator.

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:16.850 align:middle line:84%
So a fancy way of
combining 2 and 3

00:29:16.850 --> 00:29:22.050 align:middle line:84%
is that the set of
compact operators

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:27.330 align:middle line:84%
is a star closed two-sided
ideal in the algebra bounded

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:29.960 align:middle line:90%
linear operators.

00:29:29.960 --> 00:29:37.100 align:middle line:84%
But I'm just going to
list conditions, and not

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:38.610 align:middle line:90%
give fancy words for them.

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:39.580 align:middle line:90%
So 1 is clear.

00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:41.330 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to write
the proof for that.

00:29:41.330 --> 00:29:43.430 align:middle line:90%
We'll just do 2 and 3.

00:29:43.430 --> 00:29:47.450 align:middle line:84%
And although we could do it
directly from the definition,

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:51.330 align:middle line:84%
let's use the theorem that
we just worked hard to prove.

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:57.380 align:middle line:84%
So if T is a compact
operator, then

00:29:57.380 --> 00:30:01.850 align:middle line:84%
that implies that there exists
a sequence of finite rank

00:30:01.850 --> 00:30:08.450 align:middle line:84%
operators such
that T n converges

00:30:08.450 --> 00:30:11.210 align:middle line:90%
to T in operator norm.

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:17.620 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, by what
we proved last time,

00:30:17.620 --> 00:30:19.900 align:middle line:84%
we showed that if we have
a finite rank operator,

00:30:19.900 --> 00:30:26.400 align:middle line:84%
then its adjoint is also a
finite rank operator, which

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:36.920 align:middle line:84%
implies that if I take
the norm of the adjoint

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:41.800 align:middle line:84%
of this finite rank
operator and minus

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:45.310 align:middle line:84%
the adjoint of the compact
operator-- remember,

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:47.950 align:middle line:84%
we have this nice
theorem that tells us

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:50.590 align:middle line:84%
the norm of the adjoint
is equal to the norm

00:30:50.590 --> 00:30:58.180 align:middle line:84%
of the original operator-- this
is equal to T n minus T, which

00:30:58.180 --> 00:31:00.400 align:middle line:90%
we're assuming goes to 0.

00:31:00.400 --> 00:31:04.960 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
adjoint is a norm limit

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:08.420 align:middle line:90%
of finite rank operators.

00:31:08.420 --> 00:31:10.700 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, it must
be a compact operator.

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:15.660 align:middle line:90%


00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:19.800 align:middle line:84%
And to prove 3, we'll use
this characterization again.

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:27.900 align:middle line:84%
So again, assume T is a compact
operator so that there exists

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:31.680 align:middle line:84%
a sequence of T n's and
a finite rank operators

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:34.470 align:middle line:84%
converging to T and
the operator norm.

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:38.760 align:middle line:90%


00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:47.310 align:middle line:84%
So T n finite rank
operators, T n minus T

00:31:47.310 --> 00:31:55.350 align:middle line:90%
converges to 0 implies, then--

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:58.860 align:middle line:84%
now, last time, we showed
that the set of finite rank

00:31:58.860 --> 00:32:01.600 align:middle line:84%
operators also satisfies
these two conditions.

00:32:01.600 --> 00:32:04.140 align:middle line:84%
This one I just used
here, but also satisfies

00:32:04.140 --> 00:32:06.750 align:middle line:84%
this one-- that if I take
a finite rank operator

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:09.600 align:middle line:84%
and multiply it on either side
by a bounded linear operator,

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:14.770 align:middle line:84%
I still have a
finite rank operator.

00:32:14.770 --> 00:32:18.420 align:middle line:84%
So for all n, I
also have A times

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:22.450 align:middle line:84%
T n B is also a
finite rank operator

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:24.210 align:middle line:90%
by what we did last time.

00:32:24.210 --> 00:32:31.860 align:middle line:84%
And if I take the norm
of A T n B minus A T B,

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:42.330 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to the norm
of A T n minus T B. And now,

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:46.410 align:middle line:84%
the norm preserves this
kind of algebraic structure

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:48.240 align:middle line:90%
of bounded linear operators--

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:50.850 align:middle line:84%
I shouldn't say "preserves,"
but "respects" it,

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:54.300 align:middle line:84%
meaning the norm of a
product is less than

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:56.080 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the
product of the norm.

00:32:56.080 --> 00:32:57.720 align:middle line:84%
So this is less
than or equal to--

00:32:57.720 --> 00:33:01.420 align:middle line:84%
this just follows
from the definition--

00:33:01.420 --> 00:33:08.460 align:middle line:84%
T n minus T B. And so
this norm is fixed.

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:09.690 align:middle line:90%
This norm is fixed.

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:13.330 align:middle line:90%
This thing is going to 0.

00:33:13.330 --> 00:33:16.930 align:middle line:84%
So I have bounded above
this non-negative quantity

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:18.850 align:middle line:90%
by something converging to 0.

00:33:18.850 --> 00:33:22.510 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, this
thing converges to 0.

00:33:22.510 --> 00:33:25.600 align:middle line:84%
So A times T times
B is the norm limit

00:33:25.600 --> 00:33:27.490 align:middle line:84%
of a sequence of
finite rank operators.

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:28.930 align:middle line:90%
So it's also compact.

00:33:28.930 --> 00:33:45.670 align:middle line:90%


00:33:45.670 --> 00:33:50.140 align:middle line:90%
Now, coming back to--

00:33:50.140 --> 00:33:56.600 align:middle line:90%


00:33:56.600 --> 00:33:58.970 align:middle line:90%
one second.

00:33:58.970 --> 00:33:59.855 align:middle line:90%
Hydration is key.

00:33:59.855 --> 00:34:20.870 align:middle line:90%


00:34:20.870 --> 00:34:25.610 align:middle line:84%
Now, one of the
most important set

00:34:25.610 --> 00:34:32.900 align:middle line:84%
of numbers that one can
associate to a matrix

00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:43.790 align:middle line:84%
is the set of its
eigenvalues, and not just--

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:50.630 align:middle line:84%
so these eigenvalues typically
represent possibly the modes

00:34:50.630 --> 00:34:53.900 align:middle line:90%
of vibration of a string.

00:34:53.900 --> 00:34:57.710 align:middle line:84%
Or if you're taking Quantum
Mechanics, in which case

00:34:57.710 --> 00:35:01.010 align:middle line:84%
you're not necessarily dealing
with matrices, depending

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:04.370 align:middle line:84%
on how it's being taught
to you, these eigenvalues

00:35:04.370 --> 00:35:06.785 align:middle line:84%
are the energy levels
for bound states.

00:35:06.785 --> 00:35:10.240 align:middle line:90%


00:35:10.240 --> 00:35:14.530 align:middle line:84%
So perhaps, since
compact operators

00:35:14.530 --> 00:35:18.520 align:middle line:84%
are the inverses of
differential operators, which

00:35:18.520 --> 00:35:20.410 align:middle line:84%
most definitely appear
in quantum mechanics

00:35:20.410 --> 00:35:21.850 align:middle line:84%
but also in other
applications, it

00:35:21.850 --> 00:35:28.120 align:middle line:84%
would be good to develop some
sort of theory of eigenvalues

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:30.820 align:middle line:84%
and eigenvectors
for more general

00:35:30.820 --> 00:35:33.700 align:middle line:84%
bounded linear
operators, and then

00:35:33.700 --> 00:35:35.260 align:middle line:90%
specify to compact operators.

00:35:35.260 --> 00:35:38.210 align:middle line:90%
But what am I going on about?

00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:40.690 align:middle line:84%
So now, we're going to
discuss the spectrum

00:35:40.690 --> 00:35:50.480 align:middle line:84%
of a bounded linear
operator, which

00:35:50.480 --> 00:36:00.010 align:middle line:84%
is a generalization of
eigenvalues and eigenvectors

00:36:00.010 --> 00:36:03.100 align:middle line:84%
that you encountered
in linear algebra.

00:36:03.100 --> 00:36:08.840 align:middle line:90%


00:36:08.840 --> 00:36:12.990 align:middle line:84%
In linear algebra,
the spectrum will,

00:36:12.990 --> 00:36:17.760 align:middle line:84%
if I take H to be just a
finite-dimensional space of R n

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:21.570 align:middle line:84%
C n, then the spectrum, as
we're going to introduce it now,

00:36:21.570 --> 00:36:26.220 align:middle line:84%
consists entirely of the
eigenvalues of that matrix.

00:36:26.220 --> 00:36:31.990 align:middle line:84%
Not so if we move on to now
bounded linear operators, which

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:33.850 align:middle line:84%
is what makes it
pretty interesting,

00:36:33.850 --> 00:36:39.490 align:middle line:84%
is that there's more stuff
that's associated to the fact

00:36:39.490 --> 00:36:41.410 align:middle line:84%
that you're working in
infinite dimensions.

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:44.620 align:middle line:90%


00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:47.770 align:middle line:84%
And rather than harp
on about it, let's go.

00:36:47.770 --> 00:36:52.760 align:middle line:84%
Let's just dive in and start
discussing the spectrum.

00:36:52.760 --> 00:36:56.630 align:middle line:84%
First, I want to bring up a
few facts that you proved in,

00:36:56.630 --> 00:36:58.130 align:middle line:84%
I think it was the
first assignment,

00:36:58.130 --> 00:37:01.800 align:middle line:84%
first or second
assignment of the course.

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:05.660 align:middle line:84%
So throughout, again,
H is a Hilbert space.

00:37:05.660 --> 00:37:08.510 align:middle line:84%
I will say it maybe at one
point that it's separable,

00:37:08.510 --> 00:37:12.390 align:middle line:84%
but throughout, H is
always a Hilbert space.

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:17.030 align:middle line:84%
So we have the following
theorem, which tells you

00:37:17.030 --> 00:37:19.070 align:middle line:84%
how to invert certain
operators via what's

00:37:19.070 --> 00:37:20.000 align:middle line:90%
called Neumann series.

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:22.835 align:middle line:90%


00:37:22.835 --> 00:37:29.570 align:middle line:84%
Let T be a bounded linear
operator on a Hilbert space.

00:37:29.570 --> 00:37:40.430 align:middle line:84%
If the norm of T is less than
1, then the operator I minus T

00:37:40.430 --> 00:37:41.750 align:middle line:90%
is invertible.

00:37:41.750 --> 00:37:44.750 align:middle line:90%


00:37:44.750 --> 00:37:47.510 align:middle line:84%
And if I want to
compute the inverse,

00:37:47.510 --> 00:37:51.110 align:middle line:84%
I can compute it
just like I would

00:37:51.110 --> 00:37:55.430 align:middle line:90%
if I minus T was 1 minus x.

00:37:55.430 --> 00:38:00.820 align:middle line:84%
And we're talking about dividing
by the function 1 minus x.

00:38:00.820 --> 00:38:04.187 align:middle line:90%
You have a power series--

00:38:04.187 --> 00:38:05.770 align:middle line:84%
not power series,
but you can write it

00:38:05.770 --> 00:38:07.540 align:middle line:90%
as a geometric series--

00:38:07.540 --> 00:38:11.590 align:middle line:84%
sum from n equals 0 to
infinity T to the n.

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:15.170 align:middle line:84%
And this series over here
is absolutely convergent.

00:38:15.170 --> 00:38:15.670 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:18.393 align:middle line:90%
Because the norm of every term--

00:38:18.393 --> 00:38:20.560 align:middle line:84%
norm of T to the n is less
than or equal to the norm

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:22.570 align:middle line:90%
of T raised to the n.

00:38:22.570 --> 00:38:24.250 align:middle line:84%
And the norm of
t is less than 1.

00:38:24.250 --> 00:38:28.780 align:middle line:84%
So this thing is
absolutely summable.

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:32.020 align:middle line:84%
And you use that when you
actually proved this theorem

00:38:32.020 --> 00:38:32.960 align:middle line:90%
in the assignment.

00:38:32.960 --> 00:38:36.630 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going to give
the proof of it again.

00:38:36.630 --> 00:38:38.310 align:middle line:84%
I didn't give it
once before, you did,

00:38:38.310 --> 00:38:40.590 align:middle line:90%
but I'm not going to prove it.

00:38:40.590 --> 00:38:43.290 align:middle line:90%
We'll just move on.

00:38:43.290 --> 00:38:45.000 align:middle line:84%
And so another fact
that you proved

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:52.230 align:middle line:84%
using this is that
the set GL h, which

00:38:52.230 --> 00:39:01.430 align:middle line:84%
is the set of bounded linear
operators that are invertible,

00:39:01.430 --> 00:39:03.380 align:middle line:90%
meaning it's bijective--

00:39:03.380 --> 00:39:05.180 align:middle line:84%
by the open mapping
theorem, we know

00:39:05.180 --> 00:39:08.000 align:middle line:84%
that if an operator
is bijective, then

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:14.840 align:middle line:84%
it automatically has
a bounded inverse,

00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:17.780 align:middle line:90%
so I'll say bijective--

00:39:17.780 --> 00:39:25.640 align:middle line:84%
that this is an open set,
is an open subset of what?

00:39:25.640 --> 00:39:29.660 align:middle line:84%
Of the space of bounded
linear operators.

00:39:29.660 --> 00:39:33.190 align:middle line:90%


00:39:33.190 --> 00:39:40.580 align:middle line:84%
So the proof is quite quick
using this previous theorem.

00:39:40.580 --> 00:39:48.850 align:middle line:84%
So let T0 be an element of
the invertible operators on H,

00:39:48.850 --> 00:39:57.960 align:middle line:84%
GL H. So that means we
need to find an epsilon so

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:01.770 align:middle line:84%
that the ball centered
at T0 of radius epsilon

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:04.890 align:middle line:84%
is contained in GL H, meaning
everything in that ball

00:40:04.890 --> 00:40:06.030 align:middle line:90%
is invertible.

00:40:06.030 --> 00:40:10.800 align:middle line:84%
So suppose that I
have an operator T

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:20.990 align:middle line:84%
and its distance in operator
norm to T0 is less than 1

00:40:20.990 --> 00:40:23.855 align:middle line:84%
over the operator norm
of the inverse of T0.

00:40:23.855 --> 00:40:28.930 align:middle line:90%


00:40:28.930 --> 00:40:32.320 align:middle line:84%
I claim that T then
has to be invertible.

00:40:32.320 --> 00:40:34.930 align:middle line:90%


00:40:34.930 --> 00:40:44.380 align:middle line:84%
Then, if I look at T0
times T minus T0 inverse--

00:40:44.380 --> 00:40:49.060 align:middle line:84%
so t0 inverse times T
minus T0, in operator norm

00:40:49.060 --> 00:40:52.630 align:middle line:84%
that's less than or equal
to T norm of T inverse

00:40:52.630 --> 00:40:59.140 align:middle line:84%
times the norm of T minus T0 and
norm, and that's less than 1.

00:40:59.140 --> 00:41:07.300 align:middle line:84%
So by the previous theorem I get
that I minus T inverse T0 or T0

00:41:07.300 --> 00:41:14.160 align:middle line:84%
inverse applied to T
minus T0 is invertible

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:18.670 align:middle line:84%
because this thing here
has norm less than 1,

00:41:18.670 --> 00:41:22.210 align:middle line:84%
which implies that T, which
is equal to the product of two

00:41:22.210 --> 00:41:30.400 align:middle line:84%
invertible operators, namely
T0 and I minus T0 minus 1,

00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:40.880 align:middle line:84%
T minus T0, is a bijective
bounded linear operator

00:41:40.880 --> 00:41:43.310 align:middle line:84%
or a bounded linear operator
with a bounded inverse.

00:41:43.310 --> 00:41:46.570 align:middle line:90%


00:41:46.570 --> 00:41:50.400 align:middle line:90%
And so let me just summarize.

00:41:50.400 --> 00:42:04.500 align:middle line:84%
I.e., the ball of radius 1
over the norm of the inverse

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:10.170 align:middle line:84%
is contained in
the set of inverted

00:42:10.170 --> 00:42:12.540 align:middle line:90%
bounded linear operators.

00:42:12.540 --> 00:42:14.910 align:middle line:84%
And that proves that
this is an open set.

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:28.560 align:middle line:90%


00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:35.350 align:middle line:84%
So another way to think about--
so if you go back to what

00:42:35.350 --> 00:42:39.880 align:middle line:84%
eigenvalues, eigenvectors were,
it's especially clean if you

00:42:39.880 --> 00:42:42.870 align:middle line:90%
have what were--

00:42:42.870 --> 00:42:46.320 align:middle line:84%
depending on if you did
stuff with complex value

00:42:46.320 --> 00:42:52.350 align:middle line:84%
matrices or not, but let's
say they're symmetric,

00:42:52.350 --> 00:42:54.460 align:middle line:84%
and you're dealing with
real value matrices,

00:42:54.460 --> 00:42:56.850 align:middle line:84%
so matrices with
real entries, then

00:42:56.850 --> 00:43:02.220 align:middle line:84%
a theorem of linear algebra
is that you can diagonalize

00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:06.240 align:middle line:84%
that matrix, meaning you can
find an orthonormal basis

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:13.470 align:middle line:84%
of R n So that in that basis,
the matrix is simply diagonal,

00:43:13.470 --> 00:43:15.420 align:middle line:84%
and the numbers
appearing on the diagonal

00:43:15.420 --> 00:43:19.110 align:middle line:84%
are the eigenvalues
of that operator.

00:43:19.110 --> 00:43:26.980 align:middle line:84%
Now, another way to think about
that is that for which lambda,

00:43:26.980 --> 00:43:30.820 align:middle line:84%
say, can I invert the
matrix A minus lambda?

00:43:30.820 --> 00:43:36.430 align:middle line:84%
In fact, that's exactly how
the eigenvalues are defined.

00:43:36.430 --> 00:43:37.930 align:middle line:84%
If you want to find
an eigenvector,

00:43:37.930 --> 00:43:41.390 align:middle line:84%
then that should be in the
null space of A minus lambda.

00:43:41.390 --> 00:43:43.090 align:middle line:84%
So you compute the
determinant of that,

00:43:43.090 --> 00:43:45.622 align:middle line:84%
and you get a polynomial
that you saw for the lambdas.

00:43:45.622 --> 00:43:47.830 align:middle line:84%
And then you get to solve
for the eigenvectors, which

00:43:47.830 --> 00:43:51.430 align:middle line:84%
is a system of linear
equations once you know lambda.

00:43:51.430 --> 00:43:57.280 align:middle line:84%
But thinking back,
those eigenvalues

00:43:57.280 --> 00:44:06.980 align:middle line:84%
are impediments to being able
to solve the equation A minus

00:44:06.980 --> 00:44:09.080 align:middle line:90%
lambda u equals f.

00:44:09.080 --> 00:44:13.670 align:middle line:84%
And so with that
in mind, that's how

00:44:13.670 --> 00:44:18.210 align:middle line:84%
we'll define the spectrum of
a bounded linear operator.

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:23.130 align:middle line:84%
So first, I'm going to define
the complement of the spectrum.

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:26.840 align:middle line:84%
So let A be a bounded
linear operator.

00:44:26.840 --> 00:44:31.490 align:middle line:90%


00:44:31.490 --> 00:44:32.930 align:middle line:90%
The resolvent set of A--

00:44:32.930 --> 00:44:38.310 align:middle line:90%


00:44:38.310 --> 00:44:39.570 align:middle line:90%
so this is the new bit--

00:44:39.570 --> 00:44:43.560 align:middle line:90%


00:44:43.560 --> 00:44:53.970 align:middle line:84%
is a set Res A. This consists
of all complex numbers lambda,

00:44:53.970 --> 00:44:58.290 align:middle line:84%
with the property
that A minus lambda

00:44:58.290 --> 00:45:03.630 align:middle line:90%
I is an invertible operator.

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:07.460 align:middle line:90%


00:45:07.460 --> 00:45:15.490 align:middle line:84%
Now, I'm a bit lazy,
and so throughout,

00:45:15.490 --> 00:45:17.680 align:middle line:84%
instead of writing
A minus lambda I,

00:45:17.680 --> 00:45:20.025 align:middle line:84%
will most often
write A minus lambda,

00:45:20.025 --> 00:45:21.400 align:middle line:84%
with the understanding
that there

00:45:21.400 --> 00:45:24.960 align:middle line:90%
should be an identity here.

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:30.030 align:middle line:84%
So the resolvent set is a set
of all lambdas complex numbers

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:31.730 align:middle line:84%
so that A minus
lambda is invertible.

00:45:31.730 --> 00:45:33.188 align:middle line:84%
In other words,
that you can always

00:45:33.188 --> 00:45:35.180 align:middle line:84%
solve the equation
A minus lambda

00:45:35.180 --> 00:45:41.190 align:middle line:90%
u equals f for arbitrary f.

00:45:41.190 --> 00:45:47.780 align:middle line:84%
And you can solve it uniquely,
uniquely being the key.

00:45:47.780 --> 00:45:57.570 align:middle line:90%
The spectrum of A--

00:45:57.570 --> 00:46:00.600 align:middle line:84%
so this is a new
bit of terminology--

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:02.220 align:middle line:90%
is simply the complement.

00:46:02.220 --> 00:46:09.440 align:middle line:90%


00:46:09.440 --> 00:46:15.110 align:middle line:84%
So usually, we write it
Spec A equals C take away

00:46:15.110 --> 00:46:31.850 align:middle line:84%
the resolvent set of A.
So this is the example

00:46:31.850 --> 00:46:33.830 align:middle line:90%
I was saying a minute ago.

00:46:33.830 --> 00:46:43.840 align:middle line:84%
Let's say I have just a
matrix, linear transformation

00:46:43.840 --> 00:46:48.730 align:middle line:84%
of C2 to C2, and A is
just given by lambda 1, 0,

00:46:48.730 --> 00:46:53.050 align:middle line:84%
0, lambda 2, for some two
numbers lambda 1 and lambda 2.

00:46:53.050 --> 00:46:56.880 align:middle line:90%


00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:59.160 align:middle line:84%
So I'm giving you the
simplest example possible.

00:46:59.160 --> 00:47:04.410 align:middle line:84%
Then A minus lambda is equal
to lambda 1 minus lambda 0, 0,

00:47:04.410 --> 00:47:06.210 align:middle line:90%
lambda 2 minus lambda.

00:47:06.210 --> 00:47:09.210 align:middle line:84%
And when is this
matrix invertible?

00:47:09.210 --> 00:47:13.380 align:middle line:84%
Precisely when lambda does not
equal lambda 1 or lambda 2.

00:47:13.380 --> 00:47:20.450 align:middle line:90%


00:47:20.450 --> 00:47:26.570 align:middle line:84%
So A minus lambda is
invertible if and only

00:47:26.570 --> 00:47:33.650 align:middle line:84%
if lambda does not equal
lambda 1 or lambda 2.

00:47:33.650 --> 00:47:38.450 align:middle line:84%
And when lambda equals
lambda 2 or lambda 1,

00:47:38.450 --> 00:47:43.250 align:middle line:84%
this matrix here has
a non-trivial kernel.

00:47:43.250 --> 00:47:45.680 align:middle line:84%
And that's really
the only obstruction

00:47:45.680 --> 00:47:49.820 align:middle line:90%
to being invertible.

00:47:49.820 --> 00:47:51.950 align:middle line:84%
But in infinite
dimensions, when we

00:47:51.950 --> 00:47:53.780 align:middle line:84%
have operators on
infinite dimensions,

00:47:53.780 --> 00:47:59.030 align:middle line:84%
there's an additional
wrinkle that could happen.

00:47:59.030 --> 00:48:01.310 align:middle line:84%
So let me finish
with this example.

00:48:01.310 --> 00:48:08.090 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, the residual set
of A or residual resolvent set

00:48:08.090 --> 00:48:14.600 align:middle line:84%
of A equals C take away
lambda 1, lambda 2.

00:48:14.600 --> 00:48:26.150 align:middle line:84%
And the spectrum of a equals
C set lambda 1, lambda 2.

00:48:26.150 --> 00:48:34.750 align:middle line:90%


00:48:34.750 --> 00:48:36.670 align:middle line:84%
So we kind of have
a special name

00:48:36.670 --> 00:48:43.600 align:middle line:84%
for if something pops up
in the spectrum, in kind

00:48:43.600 --> 00:48:46.280 align:middle line:84%
of the same way we have
for regular matrices.

00:48:46.280 --> 00:49:03.180 align:middle line:84%
So definition-- if A is a
bounded linear operator and A

00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:05.350 align:middle line:90%
minus lambda--

00:49:05.350 --> 00:49:06.870 align:middle line:90%
so lambda is a complex number--

00:49:06.870 --> 00:49:15.540 align:middle line:84%
is not injective-- so we need
two conditions for A minus

00:49:15.540 --> 00:49:17.710 align:middle line:84%
lambda, or for lambda to
be in the resolvent set,

00:49:17.710 --> 00:49:22.380 align:middle line:84%
we need A minus lambda to be
both injective and surjective,

00:49:22.380 --> 00:49:25.170 align:middle line:90%
one to one and onto.

00:49:25.170 --> 00:49:28.005 align:middle line:84%
Then this implies that it
has non-trivial kernel.

00:49:28.005 --> 00:49:34.850 align:middle line:90%


00:49:34.850 --> 00:49:39.680 align:middle line:84%
And there exists a
u in H, take away 0,

00:49:39.680 --> 00:49:44.030 align:middle line:90%
so that A u equals lambda u.

00:49:44.030 --> 00:49:47.770 align:middle line:90%


00:49:47.770 --> 00:49:54.040 align:middle line:84%
We then call this lambda,
which is in the spectrum of A--

00:49:54.040 --> 00:49:57.340 align:middle line:84%
it's in the spectrum because
A minus lambda is not

00:49:57.340 --> 00:49:58.120 align:middle line:90%
invertible--

00:49:58.120 --> 00:50:01.640 align:middle line:90%


00:50:01.640 --> 00:50:17.450 align:middle line:84%
an eigenvalue of A,
and u an eigenvector.

00:50:17.450 --> 00:50:25.110 align:middle line:90%


00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:31.000 align:middle line:84%
So the best way to
learn about spectrum

00:50:31.000 --> 00:50:34.100 align:middle line:90%
is to do a lot of examples.

00:50:34.100 --> 00:50:37.390 align:middle line:84%
And so I only have a finite
amount of time in class

00:50:37.390 --> 00:50:40.240 align:middle line:84%
to teach you new stuff, so I
can't do too many examples.

00:50:40.240 --> 00:50:46.830 align:middle line:84%
But let's take, for example,
the operator from earlier,

00:50:46.830 --> 00:50:58.690 align:middle line:84%
T a equals a1 over 1, a2
over 2, a3 over 3, and so on,

00:50:58.690 --> 00:51:03.100 align:middle line:84%
for an element of little l2,
so a sequence in little L2.

00:51:03.100 --> 00:51:05.950 align:middle line:84%
And so this was, in
fact, a compact operator.

00:51:05.950 --> 00:51:09.740 align:middle line:90%


00:51:09.740 --> 00:51:11.890 align:middle line:84%
What would be some
eigenvalues of this?

00:51:11.890 --> 00:51:17.760 align:middle line:84%
Well, note that if
I apply T to one

00:51:17.760 --> 00:51:20.980 align:middle line:84%
of the elements of
orthonormal basis--

00:51:20.980 --> 00:51:28.890 align:middle line:84%
so let's call it
e n, where e n is

00:51:28.890 --> 00:51:34.650 align:middle line:84%
the sequence consisting of 1 in
the n-th spot and 0 otherwise.

00:51:34.650 --> 00:51:35.590 align:middle line:90%
What do I get?

00:51:35.590 --> 00:51:41.250 align:middle line:84%
I get 0, 0, except in
the n-th spot, 1 over n,

00:51:41.250 --> 00:51:48.270 align:middle line:84%
0, and so on, which
is 1 over n times e n.

00:51:48.270 --> 00:52:01.470 align:middle line:84%
So that proves that T minus 1
over n has non-trivial kernel,

00:52:01.470 --> 00:52:06.448 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, 1 over
n is an eigenvalue,

00:52:06.448 --> 00:52:07.740 align:middle line:90%
and therefore, in the spectrum.

00:52:07.740 --> 00:52:11.910 align:middle line:90%


00:52:11.910 --> 00:52:13.215 align:middle line:90%
So this was for all n.

00:52:13.215 --> 00:52:17.970 align:middle line:90%


00:52:17.970 --> 00:52:25.650 align:middle line:90%
These are eigenvalues of T.

00:52:25.650 --> 00:52:46.930 align:middle line:84%
So the spectrum contains at
least this sequence of numbers.

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:49.080 align:middle line:84%
So unlike in the
finite-dimensional case,

00:52:49.080 --> 00:52:51.930 align:middle line:84%
you can have infinitely
many eigenvalues.

00:52:51.930 --> 00:52:55.580 align:middle line:90%


00:52:55.580 --> 00:52:58.750 align:middle line:84%
So it's also possible that
you have no eigenvalues.

00:52:58.750 --> 00:53:08.750 align:middle line:90%


00:53:08.750 --> 00:53:12.000 align:middle line:84%
Let me just bring up
a small point here.

00:53:12.000 --> 00:53:16.840 align:middle line:90%


00:53:16.840 --> 00:53:19.000 align:middle line:90%
Let's look at 0.

00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:24.280 align:middle line:84%
Is 0 in the spectrum
or in the resultant?

00:53:24.280 --> 00:53:28.240 align:middle line:84%
In other words, So T minus 0,
that's just T. So I'm asking

00:53:28.240 --> 00:53:35.500 align:middle line:84%
is T itself injective
and surjective?

00:53:35.500 --> 00:53:37.090 align:middle line:90%
Is it bijective?

00:53:37.090 --> 00:53:39.790 align:middle line:84%
So first off, it should be
clear that this operator here

00:53:39.790 --> 00:53:40.990 align:middle line:90%
is injective.

00:53:40.990 --> 00:53:46.450 align:middle line:84%
If T a equals 0, then every
one of these numbers is 0,

00:53:46.450 --> 00:53:48.160 align:middle line:90%
and therefore, a has to be 0.

00:53:48.160 --> 00:53:49.870 align:middle line:90%
So T is clearly injective.

00:53:49.870 --> 00:53:52.990 align:middle line:90%


00:53:52.990 --> 00:53:59.310 align:middle line:84%
But try to convince yourself
that T cannot be surjective,

00:53:59.310 --> 00:54:01.860 align:middle line:84%
because if it was surjective,
the inverse would have to be

00:54:01.860 --> 00:54:03.720 align:middle line:90%
a bounded linear operator.

00:54:03.720 --> 00:54:05.340 align:middle line:84%
What would the
inverse have to be?

00:54:05.340 --> 00:54:10.740 align:middle line:84%
It would have to be I take the
elements of my sequence in l2,

00:54:10.740 --> 00:54:16.470 align:middle line:84%
and a1 gets multiplied by
1, a2 gets multiplied by 2,

00:54:16.470 --> 00:54:20.430 align:middle line:84%
a3 gets multiplied
by 3, and so on.

00:54:20.430 --> 00:54:24.750 align:middle line:84%
And that would not be a bounded
linear operator on little l2.

00:54:24.750 --> 00:54:31.140 align:middle line:84%
So that argument tells you
that it can't be surjective.

00:54:31.140 --> 00:54:36.030 align:middle line:84%
But what one can show is
that the range is dense,

00:54:36.030 --> 00:54:37.020 align:middle line:90%
though, in little l2.

00:54:37.020 --> 00:54:38.730 align:middle line:90%
So you can have this--

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:41.718 align:middle line:84%
something can be in
the spectrum, something

00:54:41.718 --> 00:54:43.260 align:middle line:84%
that's completely
different than what

00:54:43.260 --> 00:54:45.670 align:middle line:90%
happens in finite dimensions.

00:54:45.670 --> 00:54:53.090 align:middle line:84%
You can have the range
of T or T minus lambda,

00:54:53.090 --> 00:54:59.330 align:middle line:84%
say-- you could have the range
be dense in H, but not closed.

00:54:59.330 --> 00:55:02.510 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
operator is not surjective.

00:55:02.510 --> 00:55:06.740 align:middle line:84%
This one subtle
difference between what

00:55:06.740 --> 00:55:09.440 align:middle line:84%
happens in infinite dimensions
and finite dimensions-- that

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:12.680 align:middle line:84%
doesn't happen in finite
dimensions because the range

00:55:12.680 --> 00:55:16.100 align:middle line:84%
is going to be a subspace of
H, and in finite dimensions,

00:55:16.100 --> 00:55:18.750 align:middle line:84%
the range is a
finite-dimensional subspace,

00:55:18.750 --> 00:55:20.480 align:middle line:90%
and so it's always closed.

00:55:20.480 --> 00:55:21.950 align:middle line:84%
In infinite
dimensions, the range

00:55:21.950 --> 00:55:23.240 align:middle line:90%
does not have to be closed.

00:55:23.240 --> 00:55:25.430 align:middle line:84%
So this little
difference, though,

00:55:25.430 --> 00:55:29.150 align:middle line:84%
can result in something
being in the spectrum.

00:55:29.150 --> 00:55:34.220 align:middle line:84%
It's not always just
something's in the spectrum if

00:55:34.220 --> 00:55:35.540 align:middle line:90%
and only if it's an eigenvalue.

00:55:35.540 --> 00:55:37.380 align:middle line:90%
That is not the case.

00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:42.800 align:middle line:84%
And so for example,
and I'll probably

00:55:42.800 --> 00:55:57.290 align:middle line:84%
put this on the assignment,
but what you can verify

00:55:57.290 --> 00:56:01.700 align:middle line:84%
is that let's say I define now
an operator T from L2 of 0,

00:56:01.700 --> 00:56:14.940 align:middle line:84%
1 to L2 0, 1 by T f of
x equals x times f of x.

00:56:14.940 --> 00:56:20.977 align:middle line:90%


00:56:20.977 --> 00:56:33.990 align:middle line:84%
Then T has no,
none, eigenvalues.

00:56:33.990 --> 00:56:36.590 align:middle line:84%
So I'll probably put this
example either on the homework

00:56:36.590 --> 00:56:39.930 align:middle line:84%
or on the exam, but
it has no eigenvalues.

00:56:39.930 --> 00:56:43.490 align:middle line:84%
And you can compute
that the spectrum,

00:56:43.490 --> 00:56:50.360 align:middle line:84%
the set of all lambda so that T
minus lambda is not invertible,

00:56:50.360 --> 00:56:51.380 align:middle line:90%
equals 0, 1.

00:56:51.380 --> 00:56:54.320 align:middle line:90%


00:56:54.320 --> 00:56:55.220 align:middle line:90%
Why 0, 1?

00:56:55.220 --> 00:56:56.780 align:middle line:84%
It's not because
the domain's 0, 1,

00:56:56.780 --> 00:57:00.680 align:middle line:84%
but because that's essentially
the range of x, the function

00:57:00.680 --> 00:57:03.260 align:middle line:90%
x as x ranges from 0 to 1.

00:57:03.260 --> 00:57:06.110 align:middle line:90%


00:57:06.110 --> 00:57:09.050 align:middle line:84%
So when you move on to
infinite dimensions,

00:57:09.050 --> 00:57:10.610 align:middle line:84%
the spectrum doesn't
necessarily need

00:57:10.610 --> 00:57:13.760 align:middle line:84%
to just be the eigenvalues
of your operator.

00:57:13.760 --> 00:57:17.000 align:middle line:84%
In fact, there may
be no eigenvalues.

00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:19.200 align:middle line:84%
It can be something
that's much more subtle,

00:57:19.200 --> 00:57:24.290 align:middle line:84%
which occurs when the range is
not a closed subset, is not a--

00:57:24.290 --> 00:57:27.780 align:middle line:90%


00:57:27.780 --> 00:57:31.200 align:middle line:84%
the range can be dense in H,
but not close, and therefore,

00:57:31.200 --> 00:57:34.080 align:middle line:84%
not equal to all of H. So the
operator is not surjective.

00:57:34.080 --> 00:57:43.620 align:middle line:90%


00:57:43.620 --> 00:57:50.510 align:middle line:84%
So let's prove some
general properties

00:57:50.510 --> 00:57:52.970 align:middle line:90%
of spectrum and resolvent sets.

00:57:52.970 --> 00:57:59.370 align:middle line:84%
So let A be a bounded
linear operator.

00:57:59.370 --> 00:58:02.520 align:middle line:90%


00:58:02.520 --> 00:58:18.560 align:middle line:84%
Then spectrum of A is
a closed subset of C.

00:58:18.560 --> 00:58:27.280 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, it's contained
in the set of lambdas

00:58:27.280 --> 00:58:39.060 align:middle line:84%
in C with modulus less than
or equal to the norm of A.

00:58:39.060 --> 00:58:42.000 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, this
implies that the spectrum

00:58:42.000 --> 00:58:44.250 align:middle line:84%
is a compact subset of
the complex numbers.

00:58:44.250 --> 00:58:46.960 align:middle line:90%


00:58:46.960 --> 00:58:50.280 align:middle line:84%
So in particular,
since 1 over n is

00:58:50.280 --> 00:58:52.950 align:middle line:84%
in the spectrum of this
operator we have over here,

00:58:52.950 --> 00:58:55.860 align:middle line:84%
and 0 is the limit as n goes
to infinity of 1 over n,

00:58:55.860 --> 00:58:57.450 align:middle line:84%
this automatically,
from this result,

00:58:57.450 --> 00:59:01.110 align:middle line:84%
implies 0 is in the
spectrum of this operator.

00:59:01.110 --> 00:59:03.680 align:middle line:90%


00:59:03.680 --> 00:59:08.090 align:middle line:84%
Now of course, you can phrase
this, since the spectrum of A

00:59:08.090 --> 00:59:11.880 align:middle line:84%
is the complement of
the resolvent set of A,

00:59:11.880 --> 00:59:13.640 align:middle line:84%
you could have
phrased this theorem

00:59:13.640 --> 00:59:16.430 align:middle line:84%
in terms of the resolvent, which
is that the resolvent set of A

00:59:16.430 --> 00:59:19.670 align:middle line:84%
is open and the
resolvent set of A

00:59:19.670 --> 00:59:21.560 align:middle line:84%
contains the
complement of this set.

00:59:21.560 --> 00:59:25.630 align:middle line:84%
That's, in fact, how
we'll prove this theorem.

00:59:25.630 --> 00:59:38.630 align:middle line:84%
We'll show-- is open and that
this thing, the complement

00:59:38.630 --> 00:59:41.150 align:middle line:84%
of this set, is contained
in the resolvent of A,

00:59:41.150 --> 00:59:49.940 align:middle line:84%
and set of lambdas with modulus
bigger than the norm of A

00:59:49.940 --> 01:00:11.960 align:middle line:84%
is contained in the
resolvent set of A.

01:00:11.960 --> 01:00:13.430 align:middle line:90%
So why is the resolvent open?

01:00:13.430 --> 01:00:17.390 align:middle line:84%
Well, it follows from
the fact that GL H,

01:00:17.390 --> 01:00:21.290 align:middle line:84%
the space of bounded linear
operators that are invertible,

01:00:21.290 --> 01:00:22.490 align:middle line:90%
is an open set.

01:00:22.490 --> 01:00:28.130 align:middle line:84%
So take something
in the resolvent.

01:00:28.130 --> 01:00:30.200 align:middle line:84%
We have to show that
there's a small ball

01:00:30.200 --> 01:00:34.647 align:middle line:84%
around this complex number
so that that small ball

01:00:34.647 --> 01:00:36.230 align:middle line:84%
is contained in the
resolvent, meaning

01:00:36.230 --> 01:00:39.560 align:middle line:90%
A minus lambda is invertible.

01:00:39.560 --> 01:00:42.670 align:middle line:90%


01:00:42.670 --> 01:00:57.340 align:middle line:84%
Since GL H is open, this implies
there exists some small epsilon

01:00:57.340 --> 01:01:01.780 align:middle line:84%
such that if T is
an operator which

01:01:01.780 --> 01:01:09.220 align:middle line:84%
is close to A minus lambda
0, an operator norm,

01:01:09.220 --> 01:01:16.300 align:middle line:84%
this implies that T
is also invertible.

01:01:16.300 --> 01:01:22.870 align:middle line:90%


01:01:22.870 --> 01:01:30.040 align:middle line:84%
Then, if lambda minus lambda
0 is less than epsilon,

01:01:30.040 --> 01:01:36.940 align:middle line:84%
we get that the norm of A minus
lambda minus A0 minus lambda

01:01:36.940 --> 01:01:43.000 align:middle line:84%
0, the A's cancel and
I just get the operator

01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:47.380 align:middle line:90%
norm of lambda minus lambda 0--

01:01:47.380 --> 01:01:52.000 align:middle line:84%
and here, I should have
kept around the identities--

01:01:52.000 --> 01:01:56.200 align:middle line:84%
which just equals lambda minus
lambda 0, which we're assuming

01:01:56.200 --> 01:01:57.530 align:middle line:90%
is less than epsilon.

01:01:57.530 --> 01:02:01.660 align:middle line:84%
So this operator here
is within epsilon

01:02:01.660 --> 01:02:04.100 align:middle line:84%
of this convertible
operator here,

01:02:04.100 --> 01:02:13.550 align:middle line:84%
which guarantees, by
how we chose epsilon,

01:02:13.550 --> 01:02:17.990 align:middle line:84%
that A minus lambda
is invertible,

01:02:17.990 --> 01:02:24.950 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, lambda is
in the resolvent set of A.

01:02:24.950 --> 01:02:31.040 align:middle line:84%
So we started out with something
within epsilon distance

01:02:31.040 --> 01:02:34.790 align:middle line:84%
to lambda 0, where epsilon
was coming from this condition

01:02:34.790 --> 01:02:42.020 align:middle line:84%
here, and showed that lambda
is in the resolvent, i.e.,

01:02:42.020 --> 01:02:44.420 align:middle line:84%
the ball lambda
minus lambda 0 less

01:02:44.420 --> 01:02:50.450 align:middle line:84%
than epsilon is contained
in the resolvent set of A.

01:02:50.450 --> 01:02:53.870 align:middle line:84%
So that proves that the
resolvent set is open.

01:02:53.870 --> 01:03:00.450 align:middle line:90%


01:03:00.450 --> 01:03:03.420 align:middle line:84%
So now, let's show
this condition--

01:03:03.420 --> 01:03:05.580 align:middle line:84%
that if the absolute
value of lambda

01:03:05.580 --> 01:03:11.310 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than the norm
of A, then A minus lambda

01:03:11.310 --> 01:03:12.270 align:middle line:90%
is invertible.

01:03:12.270 --> 01:03:15.560 align:middle line:90%


01:03:15.560 --> 01:03:19.790 align:middle line:84%
So suppose lambda is
bigger than, strictly

01:03:19.790 --> 01:03:25.300 align:middle line:84%
bigger than, the norm of A. So
in particular, it's nonzero.

01:03:25.300 --> 01:03:37.740 align:middle line:84%
Then the norm of 1 over
lambda times A is less than 1,

01:03:37.740 --> 01:03:53.710 align:middle line:84%
and this implies that I minus
1 over lambda A is invertible.

01:03:53.710 --> 01:03:55.660 align:middle line:84%
Because then this
has norm less than 1.

01:03:55.660 --> 01:03:57.220 align:middle line:84%
So we can always
invert something

01:03:57.220 --> 01:04:01.960 align:middle line:84%
that is I minus that thing
that has norm less than 1,

01:04:01.960 --> 01:04:06.940 align:middle line:84%
which tells me that A minus
lambda is the product of two

01:04:06.940 --> 01:04:13.120 align:middle line:84%
invertible operators, namely
multiplication by minus lambda

01:04:13.120 --> 01:04:20.780 align:middle line:90%
and I minus 1 over lambda A.

01:04:20.780 --> 01:04:21.280 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:04:21.280 --> 01:04:22.738 align:middle line:84%
Let's make sure
everything cancels.

01:04:22.738 --> 01:04:24.500 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:24.500 --> 01:04:27.110 align:middle line:84%
So multiplication
by lambda, which

01:04:27.110 --> 01:04:30.320 align:middle line:84%
is a positive or nonzero
complex number, that's

01:04:30.320 --> 01:04:31.790 align:middle line:90%
an invertible operator.

01:04:31.790 --> 01:04:33.770 align:middle line:90%
This is an invertible operator.

01:04:33.770 --> 01:04:35.540 align:middle line:84%
Product of two
invertible operators

01:04:35.540 --> 01:04:41.330 align:middle line:84%
is invertible, so
that thing is in GL H.

01:04:41.330 --> 01:04:46.970 align:middle line:84%
So it's invertible, which
implies lambda, simply

01:04:46.970 --> 01:04:50.850 align:middle line:84%
by definition, is in
the resolvent set of A.

01:04:50.850 --> 01:04:54.860 align:middle line:84%
So we supposed that we had
something here, and showed

01:04:54.860 --> 01:04:57.890 align:middle line:84%
it's in here, which is
what we wanted to do.

01:04:57.890 --> 01:05:00.990 align:middle line:90%


01:05:00.990 --> 01:05:09.330 align:middle line:84%
So the spectrum is
a closed subset,

01:05:09.330 --> 01:05:13.635 align:middle line:84%
in fact, a compact subset,
of a set of complex numbers.

01:05:13.635 --> 01:05:16.440 align:middle line:90%


01:05:16.440 --> 01:05:18.630 align:middle line:84%
At least for this class,
that's about as much

01:05:18.630 --> 01:05:26.070 align:middle line:84%
as we can say for general
bounded linear operators

01:05:26.070 --> 01:05:27.840 align:middle line:90%
about their spectrum.

01:05:27.840 --> 01:05:30.270 align:middle line:84%
Let me just make a
small comment, also,

01:05:30.270 --> 01:05:37.800 align:middle line:84%
that if you know a
little complex analysis,

01:05:37.800 --> 01:05:43.080 align:middle line:84%
the previous proof actually
also allows you to--

01:05:43.080 --> 01:05:45.190 align:middle line:84%
so I'm just going to
state this as a fact.

01:05:45.190 --> 01:05:49.330 align:middle line:84%
One could ask, could the
spectrum ever be empty?

01:05:49.330 --> 01:05:53.940 align:middle line:84%
So that's certainly a compact
subset of complex numbers.

01:05:53.940 --> 01:05:56.170 align:middle line:90%
And the answer is no.

01:05:56.170 --> 01:05:59.020 align:middle line:84%
But this class does not
assume complex analysis,

01:05:59.020 --> 01:06:02.400 align:middle line:84%
so I can't really or
write a proof of that

01:06:02.400 --> 01:06:04.810 align:middle line:84%
and expect you to
completely follow it.

01:06:04.810 --> 01:06:07.110 align:middle line:90%
But here's the thinking--

01:06:07.110 --> 01:06:08.880 align:middle line:84%
so if you don't know
complex analysis,

01:06:08.880 --> 01:06:12.360 align:middle line:84%
just skip ahead a few
seconds, but the thinking

01:06:12.360 --> 01:06:17.050 align:middle line:84%
is this-- let's suppose
that the spectrum is empty.

01:06:17.050 --> 01:06:27.180 align:middle line:90%
Then A minus lambda applied--

01:06:27.180 --> 01:06:31.190 align:middle line:84%
so let's assume the spectrum
is empty, and take u and v that

01:06:31.190 --> 01:06:36.230 align:middle line:84%
are in the Hilbert space, apply
A minus lambda to u or A minus

01:06:36.230 --> 01:06:39.170 align:middle line:90%
lambda inverse, applied to u.

01:06:39.170 --> 01:06:40.850 align:middle line:90%
An inner product it would be.

01:06:40.850 --> 01:06:44.750 align:middle line:84%
So this is well defined because
A minus lambda inverse always

01:06:44.750 --> 01:06:48.560 align:middle line:84%
exists because the
spectrum is empty.

01:06:48.560 --> 01:06:51.230 align:middle line:84%
And this gives you a
continuous function

01:06:51.230 --> 01:06:55.160 align:middle line:90%
in lambda on the complex plane.

01:06:55.160 --> 01:06:56.790 align:middle line:90%
And now what happens?

01:06:56.790 --> 01:07:00.950 align:middle line:84%
So the proof, or at least one
of the estimates I gave before,

01:07:00.950 --> 01:07:10.360 align:middle line:84%
says that as the magnitude
of lambda gets large,

01:07:10.360 --> 01:07:12.460 align:middle line:84%
the operator norm
of A minus lambda

01:07:12.460 --> 01:07:18.140 align:middle line:84%
is bounded by a constant divided
by the magnitude of lambda,

01:07:18.140 --> 01:07:19.870 align:middle line:90%
which is going to 0.

01:07:19.870 --> 01:07:24.700 align:middle line:84%
So the operator norm of the
inverse of A minus lambda

01:07:24.700 --> 01:07:31.540 align:middle line:84%
goes to 0 as the magnitude
of lambda goes to infinity.

01:07:31.540 --> 01:07:36.210 align:middle line:84%
But you can show a bit
more about this function

01:07:36.210 --> 01:07:39.090 align:middle line:84%
I defined a minute ago
involving two vectors--

01:07:39.090 --> 01:07:42.570 align:middle line:84%
that basically, by this
Neumann series calculation

01:07:42.570 --> 01:07:44.910 align:middle line:84%
that in fact, that's a
complex differentiable

01:07:44.910 --> 01:07:47.630 align:middle line:90%
function in lambda.

01:07:47.630 --> 01:07:51.020 align:middle line:84%
And thus, you have this
complex differentiable function

01:07:51.020 --> 01:07:57.040 align:middle line:84%
defined on the entire plane
that goes to 0 as lambda

01:07:57.040 --> 01:07:59.480 align:middle line:90%
goes to infinity.

01:07:59.480 --> 01:08:01.480 align:middle line:90%
And by Liouville's theorem--

01:08:01.480 --> 01:08:03.010 align:middle line:84%
not Louisville,
Louisville's a type

01:08:03.010 --> 01:08:07.850 align:middle line:84%
of bat, a bat that
you hit stuff with,

01:08:07.850 --> 01:08:11.310 align:middle line:84%
not the bat that
gives you COVID--

01:08:11.310 --> 01:08:15.120 align:middle line:84%
then A minus lambda inverse
applied to u inner product v

01:08:15.120 --> 01:08:18.359 align:middle line:84%
has to be identically 0 because
that's a complex differentiable

01:08:18.359 --> 01:08:20.609 align:middle line:84%
function in lambda,
which goes to 0 as lambda

01:08:20.609 --> 01:08:22.000 align:middle line:90%
goes to infinity.

01:08:22.000 --> 01:08:23.910 align:middle line:84%
And since this applies
for all u and v,

01:08:23.910 --> 01:08:27.359 align:middle line:84%
this implies that the operator
A minus lambda inverse

01:08:27.359 --> 01:08:28.350 align:middle line:90%
is identically 0.

01:08:28.350 --> 01:08:29.529 align:middle line:90%
That's a contradiction.

01:08:29.529 --> 01:08:33.680 align:middle line:90%
So the spectrum cannot be empty.

01:08:33.680 --> 01:08:41.850 align:middle line:84%
So it will be very
useful to have

01:08:41.850 --> 01:08:43.439 align:middle line:90%
a different characterization.

01:08:43.439 --> 01:08:47.490 align:middle line:84%
So now, we're going to
specialize to not just looking

01:08:47.490 --> 01:08:51.390 align:middle line:84%
at the spectrum of
arbitrary bounded operators,

01:08:51.390 --> 01:08:55.649 align:middle line:84%
but now, we're going to zero
in on self-adjoint operators.

01:08:55.649 --> 01:08:58.340 align:middle line:90%


01:08:58.340 --> 01:09:03.290 align:middle line:84%
And first, we need
a little theorem,

01:09:03.290 --> 01:09:06.859 align:middle line:84%
that if I have a
self-adjoint operator,

01:09:06.859 --> 01:09:14.510 align:middle line:84%
meaning A is equal to A star,
then two things are true--

01:09:14.510 --> 01:09:17.090 align:middle line:90%


01:09:17.090 --> 01:09:25.910 align:middle line:84%
for all u in H, the inner
product A u applied to u

01:09:25.910 --> 01:09:28.310 align:middle line:90%
is a real number.

01:09:28.310 --> 01:09:31.430 align:middle line:84%
And two, I can give an
alternative characterization

01:09:31.430 --> 01:09:36.800 align:middle line:84%
of the norm of A,
that the norm of A

01:09:36.800 --> 01:09:40.520 align:middle line:84%
is, in fact, equal to
the sup over norm of u

01:09:40.520 --> 01:09:46.142 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 of the absolute
value of A u applied to u.

01:09:46.142 --> 01:09:48.350 align:middle line:84%
So this actually has nothing
to do with the spectrum.

01:09:48.350 --> 01:09:52.580 align:middle line:84%
This is just a
fact I'll need when

01:09:52.580 --> 01:09:58.550 align:middle line:84%
we start studying the spectrum
of self-adjoint operators.

01:09:58.550 --> 01:10:01.020 align:middle line:84%
And that will be in
the next lecture.

01:10:01.020 --> 01:10:03.380 align:middle line:84%
So with the remainder
of this lecture,

01:10:03.380 --> 01:10:05.700 align:middle line:90%
we'll prove this theorem.

01:10:05.700 --> 01:10:08.960 align:middle line:90%
So proof-- so 1 is quite easy.

01:10:08.960 --> 01:10:13.680 align:middle line:90%


01:10:13.680 --> 01:10:18.330 align:middle line:84%
If u is in H, and I look
at A u applied to u,

01:10:18.330 --> 01:10:20.010 align:middle line:84%
and I take its
complex conjugate,

01:10:20.010 --> 01:10:22.860 align:middle line:84%
something's real if and only
if its complex conjugate

01:10:22.860 --> 01:10:24.900 align:middle line:90%
is equal to the number again.

01:10:24.900 --> 01:10:27.120 align:middle line:84%
So I want to show that
the complex conjugate

01:10:27.120 --> 01:10:31.390 align:middle line:84%
of A u applied to u is
equal to A u applied to u.

01:10:31.390 --> 01:10:34.830 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal to,
by the properties

01:10:34.830 --> 01:10:39.900 align:middle line:84%
of the inner product,
u applied to A u.

01:10:39.900 --> 01:10:42.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, A is equal
to its own adjoint

01:10:42.960 --> 01:10:45.930 align:middle line:84%
so this is equal
to u applied to--

01:10:45.930 --> 01:10:48.120 align:middle line:90%
or u inner product A star u.

01:10:48.120 --> 01:10:54.020 align:middle line:84%
And by the definition of the
adjoint, this moves over here.

01:10:54.020 --> 01:10:56.970 align:middle line:90%
And that's the end.

01:10:56.970 --> 01:10:59.640 align:middle line:84%
Now, I keep bringing
up quantum mechanics,

01:10:59.640 --> 01:11:08.460 align:middle line:84%
but I am a researcher in
quantum mechanics, but I'm not.

01:11:08.460 --> 01:11:14.250 align:middle line:84%
But in quantum mechanics,
the observables--

01:11:14.250 --> 01:11:16.470 align:middle line:84%
meaning the things
that one would actually

01:11:16.470 --> 01:11:21.482 align:middle line:84%
measure like position,
momentum, center of mass,

01:11:21.482 --> 01:11:22.440 align:middle line:90%
these types of things--

01:11:22.440 --> 01:11:25.070 align:middle line:90%


01:11:25.070 --> 01:11:29.100 align:middle line:84%
are modeled by
self-adjoint operators.

01:11:29.100 --> 01:11:30.980 align:middle line:84%
These are not bounded
linear operators.

01:11:30.980 --> 01:11:34.430 align:middle line:84%
They're unbounded, which we
will not cover in this class,

01:11:34.430 --> 01:11:37.100 align:middle line:90%
but they're self adjoint.

01:11:37.100 --> 01:11:40.470 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, by this first
property this quantity here,

01:11:40.470 --> 01:11:44.040 align:middle line:84%
which is the expectation
of that measurement--

01:11:44.040 --> 01:11:45.890 align:middle line:84%
so what this number
1 tells you is

01:11:45.890 --> 01:11:48.350 align:middle line:84%
that the expectation
of this measurement

01:11:48.350 --> 01:11:49.440 align:middle line:90%
is always a real number.

01:11:49.440 --> 01:11:53.340 align:middle line:84%
It's always something that
you can measure in nature.

01:11:53.340 --> 01:11:59.450 align:middle line:84%
I mean, as far as I know, the
things one measures in nature,

01:11:59.450 --> 01:12:03.890 align:middle line:84%
not necessarily uses
to model nature,

01:12:03.890 --> 01:12:05.340 align:middle line:90%
are not complex numbers.

01:12:05.340 --> 01:12:11.510 align:middle line:84%
So number 2, let's
verify that I can,

01:12:11.510 --> 01:12:14.690 align:middle line:84%
for a self-adjoint operator,
write the norm in this way.

01:12:14.690 --> 01:12:23.820 align:middle line:90%


01:12:23.820 --> 01:12:28.365 align:middle line:90%
So let little a be this thing.

01:12:28.365 --> 01:12:36.910 align:middle line:90%


01:12:36.910 --> 01:12:39.820 align:middle line:84%
First thing to note is actually,
this is a finite number,

01:12:39.820 --> 01:12:43.870 align:middle line:84%
and it's bounded above
by the norm of A.

01:12:43.870 --> 01:12:53.070 align:middle line:84%
Note that for all
unit length vectors,

01:12:53.070 --> 01:12:57.060 align:middle line:84%
the absolute value of the
inner product of A u with u

01:12:57.060 --> 01:13:00.210 align:middle line:84%
via Cauchy-Schwarz is less
than or equal to the norm,

01:13:00.210 --> 01:13:02.730 align:middle line:84%
the product of the
norms of A u and u.

01:13:02.730 --> 01:13:05.670 align:middle line:84%
u has norm 1, so
this is less than

01:13:05.670 --> 01:13:07.470 align:middle line:90%
or equal to the norm of A u.

01:13:07.470 --> 01:13:09.720 align:middle line:84%
And by the definition of
the operator norm of A,

01:13:09.720 --> 01:13:18.210 align:middle line:84%
that's less than or equal
to the operator norm of A.

01:13:18.210 --> 01:13:19.410 align:middle line:90%
So this proves two things.

01:13:19.410 --> 01:13:22.830 align:middle line:84%
This thing on the right-hand
side is a finite number

01:13:22.830 --> 01:13:26.070 align:middle line:84%
and it's bounded above
by the norm of A.

01:13:26.070 --> 01:13:31.215 align:middle line:84%
So little a, the sup, is less
than or equal to norm of A.

01:13:31.215 --> 01:13:34.890 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll do is prove
the opposite inequality.

01:13:34.890 --> 01:13:43.840 align:middle line:90%


01:13:43.840 --> 01:13:47.570 align:middle line:84%
And to do that, we'll use this
first property, that for all u,

01:13:47.570 --> 01:13:49.600 align:middle line:90%
A u applied to u is real.

01:13:49.600 --> 01:14:00.070 align:middle line:84%
So I want to somehow
estimate the operator

01:14:00.070 --> 01:14:05.530 align:middle line:84%
norm of A, which means I need to
estimate the norm of A u, where

01:14:05.530 --> 01:14:06.730 align:middle line:90%
the norm of u equals 1.

01:14:06.730 --> 01:14:10.300 align:middle line:84%
So let's take something
in H with norm 1.

01:14:10.300 --> 01:14:13.150 align:middle line:90%


01:14:13.150 --> 01:14:18.370 align:middle line:84%
And since the
operator norm of A--

01:14:18.370 --> 01:14:19.570 align:middle line:90%
so let me just--

01:14:19.570 --> 01:14:22.520 align:middle line:90%


01:14:22.520 --> 01:14:32.450 align:middle line:84%
now what we want to show is
that the norm of A is less than

01:14:32.450 --> 01:14:33.980 align:middle line:84%
or equal to this
number, little a.

01:14:33.980 --> 01:14:41.480 align:middle line:90%


01:14:41.480 --> 01:14:43.070 align:middle line:84%
So let's take
something with norm 1.

01:14:43.070 --> 01:14:47.310 align:middle line:90%


01:14:47.310 --> 01:14:48.945 align:middle line:84%
And so that when
I hit it with A,

01:14:48.945 --> 01:14:52.350 align:middle line:84%
I don't get 0, because
the operator norm of A

01:14:52.350 --> 01:14:59.370 align:middle line:84%
is computed by taking the
sup of norms of A u, where

01:14:59.370 --> 01:15:00.780 align:middle line:90%
u has unit length.

01:15:00.780 --> 01:15:03.870 align:middle line:84%
And I just need to estimate
those A u's that are nonzero.

01:15:03.870 --> 01:15:09.360 align:middle line:90%


01:15:09.360 --> 01:15:21.210 align:middle line:84%
Let v be the vector A
u over the norm of A u.

01:15:21.210 --> 01:15:23.220 align:middle line:90%
Then this thing has unit length.

01:15:23.220 --> 01:15:33.060 align:middle line:90%


01:15:33.060 --> 01:15:37.020 align:middle line:84%
And so now, if I
compute the norm

01:15:37.020 --> 01:15:42.630 align:middle line:84%
of A u, which is the thing I
want to estimate above by a,

01:15:42.630 --> 01:15:48.710 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to
A u applied to v,

01:15:48.710 --> 01:15:51.840 align:middle line:84%
or A u inner product
v, because then I

01:15:51.840 --> 01:15:54.180 align:middle line:84%
would get A u inner
product A u, which

01:15:54.180 --> 01:15:56.790 align:middle line:84%
gives me norm of A u squared
divided by the norm of A u.

01:15:56.790 --> 01:15:59.730 align:middle line:90%
So I just get A u.

01:15:59.730 --> 01:16:06.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is a real
number because it's

01:16:06.270 --> 01:16:07.110 align:middle line:90%
equal to this norm.

01:16:07.110 --> 01:16:12.810 align:middle line:84%
So I can throw in a real part
and not get into any trouble.

01:16:12.810 --> 01:16:18.365 align:middle line:90%
And now, I have a--

01:16:18.365 --> 01:16:22.072 align:middle line:90%


01:16:22.072 --> 01:16:23.530 align:middle line:84%
let's see, what
would I call that--

01:16:23.530 --> 01:16:26.100 align:middle line:90%


01:16:26.100 --> 01:16:27.210 align:middle line:90%
polarization identity.

01:16:27.210 --> 01:16:29.910 align:middle line:84%
That's the name of that,
not the parallelogram law.

01:16:29.910 --> 01:16:33.780 align:middle line:84%
But I have a type of
polarization identity

01:16:33.780 --> 01:16:35.610 align:middle line:84%
for this expression,
which you can just

01:16:35.610 --> 01:16:41.340 align:middle line:84%
verify in the quiet
of your own dwelling

01:16:41.340 --> 01:16:48.840 align:middle line:84%
that this thing right here, I
can write as 1/4 A u plus v, u

01:16:48.840 --> 01:17:02.640 align:middle line:84%
plus v, minus A u minus
v, u minus v plus i

01:17:02.640 --> 01:17:16.882 align:middle line:84%
times A u plus i v, u
plus i v minus A u minus i

01:17:16.882 --> 01:17:34.040 align:middle line:90%
v v minus i v.

01:17:34.040 --> 01:17:37.370 align:middle line:90%
Now, so A u v--

01:17:37.370 --> 01:17:39.530 align:middle line:84%
this inner product here
is equal to this thing,

01:17:39.530 --> 01:17:40.950 align:middle line:90%
this expression I have here.

01:17:40.950 --> 01:17:42.630 align:middle line:90%
The real part is still there.

01:17:42.630 --> 01:17:45.470 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm taking the real
part of this number.

01:17:45.470 --> 01:17:48.140 align:middle line:90%
Now, this number here is real.

01:17:48.140 --> 01:17:52.100 align:middle line:84%
This number here is
real by number 1.

01:17:52.100 --> 01:17:55.730 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, i times this
number is purely imaginary.

01:17:55.730 --> 01:17:58.470 align:middle line:84%
So when I take the real
part, it goes away.

01:17:58.470 --> 01:18:03.170 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal
to 1/4 times A u

01:18:03.170 --> 01:18:19.000 align:middle line:84%
plus v minus A u
minus v, u minus v.

01:18:19.000 --> 01:18:20.260 align:middle line:90%
I'm missing something there.

01:18:20.260 --> 01:18:38.430 align:middle line:90%


01:18:38.430 --> 01:18:46.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is A
applied to a vector

01:18:46.960 --> 01:18:48.950 align:middle line:84%
inner product with
that same vector.

01:18:48.950 --> 01:18:52.930 align:middle line:84%
So that's less than
or equal to 1/4--

01:18:52.930 --> 01:18:56.110 align:middle line:84%
since little a is a supremum
of the absolute values

01:18:56.110 --> 01:18:58.750 align:middle line:84%
of these expressions
where, as long

01:18:58.750 --> 01:19:00.430 align:middle line:84%
as these things
have unit length,

01:19:00.430 --> 01:19:05.770 align:middle line:84%
this is less than or equal
to a times norm of u plus v

01:19:05.770 --> 01:19:08.260 align:middle line:90%
squared, this first expression.

01:19:08.260 --> 01:19:10.990 align:middle line:84%
Because this is less than or
equal to the absolute value

01:19:10.990 --> 01:19:12.160 align:middle line:90%
of this thing.

01:19:12.160 --> 01:19:16.000 align:middle line:84%
And dividing by u
plus v squared, this,

01:19:16.000 --> 01:19:18.040 align:middle line:84%
I get that that's less
than or equal to a.

01:19:18.040 --> 01:19:21.010 align:middle line:84%
But I still have this u plus
v squared hanging around.

01:19:21.010 --> 01:19:23.050 align:middle line:84%
And then the same thing
with this u minus v,

01:19:23.050 --> 01:19:26.830 align:middle line:90%
so plus a u minus v squared.

01:19:26.830 --> 01:19:29.930 align:middle line:90%


01:19:29.930 --> 01:19:32.800 align:middle line:84%
And now, I use the
parallelogram law.

01:19:32.800 --> 01:19:36.790 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to a
over 4, u plus v norm

01:19:36.790 --> 01:19:39.640 align:middle line:84%
squared plus u minus
v norm squared.

01:19:39.640 --> 01:19:44.680 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to 2 norm
u squared plus 2 norm v

01:19:44.680 --> 01:19:47.950 align:middle line:90%
squared times a over 4.

01:19:47.950 --> 01:19:51.020 align:middle line:90%
u and v have unit length.

01:19:51.020 --> 01:19:52.870 align:middle line:90%
So this is 1, this is 1.

01:19:52.870 --> 01:19:55.250 align:middle line:90%
So I get 2 plus 2 is 4.

01:19:55.250 --> 01:19:58.470 align:middle line:90%
And I get a

01:19:58.470 --> 01:20:05.800 align:middle line:84%
So thus, for all
norm 1 vectors, I've

01:20:05.800 --> 01:20:11.380 align:middle line:84%
proven that the norm of A u
is less than or equal to a.

01:20:11.380 --> 01:20:13.870 align:middle line:84%
I've done it just for those
so that A u is nonzero,

01:20:13.870 --> 01:20:16.660 align:middle line:84%
but I still have this
inequality of A u equals 0.

01:20:16.660 --> 01:20:21.940 align:middle line:84%
So I get that the norm of A
is less than or equal to a,

01:20:21.940 --> 01:20:24.460 align:middle line:90%
which is what I wanted to do.

01:20:24.460 --> 01:20:35.750 align:middle line:84%
So next time, we will
get into some properties

01:20:35.750 --> 01:20:47.880 align:middle line:84%
of the spectrum of self-adjoint
operators a little more,

01:20:47.880 --> 01:20:51.930 align:middle line:84%
in particular, the type of
subset of complex numbers

01:20:51.930 --> 01:20:52.500 align:middle line:90%
it has to be.

01:20:52.500 --> 01:20:55.830 align:middle line:84%
In fact, we'll find out that
the spectrum of a self-adjoint

01:20:55.830 --> 01:20:59.860 align:middle line:84%
operator has to be
contained in the real line--

01:20:59.860 --> 01:21:04.950 align:middle line:84%
so you can't have anything
with a nonzero imaginary part--

01:21:04.950 --> 01:21:10.920 align:middle line:84%
that's contained within
certain bounds related

01:21:10.920 --> 01:21:16.350 align:middle line:84%
to this type of expression,
A u applied to u.

01:21:16.350 --> 01:21:22.080 align:middle line:84%
And this also gives
us an interesting way

01:21:22.080 --> 01:21:26.010 align:middle line:84%
of checking whether the
spectrum is contained

01:21:26.010 --> 01:21:29.887 align:middle line:90%
within two real numbers.

01:21:29.887 --> 01:21:31.470 align:middle line:84%
In particular, it'll
tell us something

01:21:31.470 --> 01:21:34.830 align:middle line:84%
about when a self-adjoint
operator is non-negative,

01:21:34.830 --> 01:21:38.820 align:middle line:84%
meaning A u inner product
u is always non-negative.

01:21:38.820 --> 01:21:40.290 align:middle line:84%
And from there,
we'll start talking

01:21:40.290 --> 01:21:44.600 align:middle line:84%
about the spectral theory for
compact self-adjoint operators.

01:21:44.600 --> 01:21:46.350 align:middle line:84%
So this is kind of the
most complete thing

01:21:46.350 --> 01:21:51.450 align:middle line:84%
we can say about the spectrum
of a certain class of operators.

01:21:51.450 --> 01:21:55.800 align:middle line:84%
And what we can say about
self-adjoint compact operators

01:21:55.800 --> 01:22:00.090 align:middle line:84%
is that it's pretty close to
the finite-dimensional case--

01:22:00.090 --> 01:22:08.410 align:middle line:84%
namely, that the spectrum
is that these operators can

01:22:08.410 --> 01:22:09.940 align:middle line:90%
essentially be diagonalized.

01:22:09.940 --> 01:22:12.580 align:middle line:84%
You can find an orthonormal
basis consisting entirely

01:22:12.580 --> 01:22:16.330 align:middle line:84%
of eigenvectors
for the operator.

01:22:16.330 --> 01:22:18.810 align:middle line:90%
And we'll stop there.

01:22:18.810 --> 01:22:24.000 align:middle line:90%